[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: One export becomes the snapshot and two queryable tables

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SDOC-BOARD-ADAPTER
TITLE: One export becomes the snapshot and two queryable tables
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
adapter.py's adapt() turns one workspace.export index.json into both browser
payloads in a single pass, joining two sources the export does not carry: the
UID-to-file walk (view-check's uid_paths, imported rather than duplicated) for
provenance paths, and parse_sgra for field kinds and option lists, since the
export types every field as String. The snapshot (sdoc-board/2) is one record
per node plus one edge per RESOLVED Parent or Child declaration, with
unresolved targets kept as diagnostics rather than dropped. The rows payload
(sdoc-perspective/2) is two tables: nodes, one row per node with PATH, DIR and
degree facets; and relations, one row per declared relation, denormalized with
both endpoints' type, title, state and path. Every row of a table carries the
union key set with nulls for absent fields, because Perspective infers its
column schema from the rows.

A File relation may name part of a file rather than the whole of it, and both
payloads carry that grain: ELEMENT and ID for a named item, or a LINE_RANGE,
travel onto the relation row and onto the node's own fileRelations list. One
thing beside them is not in the export at all -- the extractor's KIND word for
the named item, such as option, module or binding -- and the adapter joins it
by asking dev/scripts/sdoc_extractors for the items of that file. That lookup
is optional by construction. The board is served and tested by a plain
python3, so an extractor that cannot be imported, or a file no configured glob
covers, leaves kind null and the card falls back to the ELEMENT word.
Reporting that an ID names nothing is element-check.py's job, not the board's;
a null kind here means "unknown", never "wrong".

The snapshot carries a third top-level key, semantics, and the adapter does
not compute it: the caller passes the sdoc-semantics/1 payload in. When none
arrives the adapter writes an explicit refusal record naming the reason rather
than omitting the key, because a missing key gives a reader no null to branch
on and reads as a settled empty answer.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The spike squashed relations into comma-joined facet strings on the node row;
Perspective cannot explode a string back into rows, so relations were
unqueryable (the operator's report, 2026-09-01). The denormalized second table
is the fix.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Updated 2026-09-03 for commits 6fbaa76b, 7d6886d1 and 46cd3cf1: File
relations became element-grained in both payloads, the KIND word is joined
from the extractor, and the snapshot gained the semantics key.
<<<
